      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The given equation is $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nSince the secant of an angle is its reciprocal of the cosine of an angle, and the tangent of an angle is the sine of an angle divided by the cosine of an angle, we can rewrite the equation as $\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nCombining the fractions on the left side gives us $\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nMultiplying both sides by $\\cos x$ gives $\\cos x + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x$.\nWe can then subtract $\\cos x$ from both sides to get $\\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x - \\cos x$.\nSimplifying this expression gives $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{3} \\cos x$.\nSince $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$, we can substitute $\\sin x$ with $\\frac{1}{3} \\cos x$ to get $\\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\cos x \\right)^2 + \\cos^2 x = 1$.\nSimplifying this equation gives $\\frac{1}{9} \\cos^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$.\nMultiplying both sides by 9 gives $\\cos^2 x + 9 \\cos^2 x = 9$.\nCombining like terms gives $10 \\cos^2 x = 9$.\nDividing both sides by 10 gives $\\cos^2 x = \\frac{9}{10}$.\nTaking the square root of both sides gives $\\cos x = \\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$.\nSubstituting this value back into the equation $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{3} \\cos x$ gives $\\sin x = \\frac{\\pm 1}{3} \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$.\nSimplifying this expression gives $\\sin x = \\boxed{\\frac{\\pm 1}{\\sqrt{10}}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{\\pm1}{\\sqrt{10}}",
