      "text": "To solve for $\\sin x$ given that $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}$, we start by using the fundamental definitions and identities of trigonometric functions.\n\nFirstly, recall the definitions of $\\sec x$ and $\\tan x$:\n\\[\n\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}, \\quad \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}\\), we can substitute the definitions:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\]\n\nCombine the fractions on the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x.\n\\]\n\nNext, square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\\[\n(1 + \\sin x)^2 = \\left(\\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\\right)^2.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side with \\(\\vec{A^2} = \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{9}\\):\n\\[\n\\left(1 + \\sin x\\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{9} \\cos^2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos^2 x = 1 - \\sin^2 x\\):\n\\[\n\\left(1 + \\sin x\\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{9} (1 - \\sin^2 x).\n\\]\n\nExpand the left-hand side:\n\\[\n1 + 2\\sin x + \\sin^2 x = \\frac{16}{9} - \\frac{16}{9} \\sin^2 x.\n\\]\n\nCombine all terms on one side of the equation:\n\\[\n1 + 2\\sin x + \\sin^2 x + \\frac{16}{9} \\sin^2 x - \\frac{16}{9} = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 2\\sin x + \\left(1 + \\frac{16}{9} \\right) \\sin^2 x = \\frac{16}{9},\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 2\\sin x + \\frac{25}{9} \\sin^2 x = 0.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(y = \\sin x\\):\n\\[\ny^2 + 2 \\left(y + \\frac{25}{9}\\right)y +  \\left(1 + \\frac{25}{9}\\right)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n9y^2 + 18y +25y+ 34 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n9y^2 + 43y + 34 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolve utilize the quadratic equation \\(a y^2 + b y + c = 0\\) where \\(a = 9\\), \\(b = 43\\), and \\(c = 34\\).\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{ b^2 - 4ac }}{2a}.\n\\]\n\nGet the discriminant \\(D = b^2 - 4ac\\):\n\\[\nD = 43^2 - 4 \\times 9 \\times 34\n\\]\n\n\\[\nD = 1849 - 1128,\n\\]\n\n\\[\nD = 721.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-43 \\pm \\sqrt{721}}{18}.\n\\]\n\nUsing Pythagorean triangle, we will verify these values yielding thus giving \\( \\sin y = -\\frac{13}{18}, \\frac{13}{18}\\).\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{13}{18} \\text{ and } \\frac{13}{18}}\n\\]",
